      brandExamples: ["NARCAN", "KLOXXADO", "RIVIVE", "REXTOVY", "REZENOPY", "ZIMHI"],
      class: "Short-acting opioid-receptor antagonist and emergency opioid-overdose reversal agent",
      usedToTreat: "Known or suspected opioid overdose with respiratory or central nervous system depression. Naloxone buys time by reversing opioid effect, but it does not replace ventilation, CPR, emergency evaluation, or treatment of non-opioid causes of unresponsiveness.",
      description: "Naloxone is a competitive opioid-receptor antagonist used when opioid effect is suppressing breathing or consciousness. Product identity matters because Narcan 4 mg/0.1 mL, Kloxxado 8 mg/0.1 mL, RiVive 3 mg/0.1 mL, Rextovy 4 mg/0.25 mL, Rezenopy 10 mg/0.11 mL nasal products and Zimhi 5 mg/0.5 mL intramuscular or subcutaneous injection are different single-use delivery systems rather than interchangeable volume instructions. A response supports opioid involvement, but absent response does not exclude fentanyl exposure, delayed absorption, hypoxia, or another emergency. Rextovy received FDA over-the-counter approval in June 2026; access and labeling differ across products.",
      mechanism: "Naloxone competes with opioids at mu and other opioid receptors, displacing agonist signaling so respiratory drive and airway protection can recover. Its receptor effect can wear off before a long-acting, high-potency, or repeatedly absorbed opioid clears; therefore recurrent sedation is possible even after an initially dramatic response. Partial agonists such as buprenorphine bind tightly, so ventilation and repeated antagonist assessment may be more important than expecting one device to produce complete awakening. The clinical goal is adequate breathing, not forced full arousal, because abrupt complete reversal can trigger severe withdrawal and sympathetic stress.",
