      nursingEssentials: ["Check breathing, pulse, oxygenation, responsiveness, exposure history, glucose, and co-ingestants while supporting the airway because naloxone corrects opioid receptor depression but not hypoglycemia, stroke, trauma, or sedative co-ingestion.", "Document the exact product, strength, route, time, response, and every repeat because nominal milligrams and device volumes differ.", "Place the breathing patient in a recovery position when appropriate and prepare suction and ventilation because vomiting and abrupt withdrawal can threaten the airway."],
      keyLabs: ["Do not wait for a toxicology result or opioid concentration before giving naloxone because prolonged hypoventilation causes hypoxic injury and bedside rescue is a clinical decision.", "Trend respiratory rate, chest movement, pulse oximetry, and capnography when available, and obtain a blood gas when ventilation remains impaired because oxygen saturation alone can look acceptable while carbon dioxide rises.", "Check point-of-care glucose and use ECG, electrolytes, renal and hepatic tests, toxicology studies, and complication-directed imaging as indicated because hypoglycemia, co-ingestants, organ failure, aspiration, trauma, or stroke can explain persistent coma or change recurrence risk."],
      adverseEffects: ["Abrupt receptor displacement can precipitate acute withdrawal with agitation, pain, vomiting, diarrhea, hypertension, and tachycardia because a dependent nervous system suddenly loses opioid signaling.", "Vomiting, agitation, and rapid return of pain can cause aspiration, falls, line removal, or injury, so protect the airway and use the breathing response rather than forced full arousal as the monitored endpoint.", "Pulmonary edema, ventricular dysrhythmia, blood-pressure instability, seizure, or cardiac arrest have been reported after abrupt reversal, particularly with cardiovascular disease or other cardiotoxic drugs, so monitor high-risk patients because sympathetic stress and the preceding hypoxic event may both contribute."],
      contraindications: ["Known hypersensitivity to naloxone or a product ingredient is the labeled contraindication because re-exposure can provoke another hypersensitivity reaction.", "Opioid dependence, pregnancy, or chronic pain is not a reason to withhold emergency reversal when breathing is failing; use immediate risk-benefit judgment because untreated patient or maternal hypoxia is more dangerous, while abrupt withdrawal still requires monitoring.", "Do not use naloxone response as proof that every coma is opioid-only or as a substitute for ventilation and emergency care because it does not reverse hypoglycemia, stroke, trauma, or non-opioid sedatives."],
      escalationRecurrence: ["No response after initial and repeat labeled doses, recurrent slowing of breathing, pulmonary edema, severe agitation, chest pain, seizure, or persistent coma requires immediate advanced airway, toxicology, and emergency evaluation."],
      interactions: ["Methadone, sustained-release opioids, large fentanyl or analog exposure with ongoing absorption, or body-packed drug can outlast naloxone, so initial improvement does not prevent recurrent respiratory depression.", "Buprenorphine and pentazocine may be incompletely or gradually reversed because tight receptor binding or mixed agonist-antagonist behavior reduces the response to one naloxone dose.", "Benzodiazepines, alcohol, xylazine, gabapentinoids, and other non-opioid depressants remain active because naloxone only antagonizes opioid receptors, so persistent sedation still needs airway support and a broader differential."],
      populationRisks: [{ population: "Infants and children", note: "Use the labeled pediatric-capable product and airway support; sudden withdrawal can include seizures or excessive crying, and very small patients still need emergency monitoring." }, { population: "Pregnancy", note: "Maternal hypoxia threatens both patients, while abrupt withdrawal may stress the fetus; emergency reversal and obstetric assessment should not be delayed because restoring maternal oxygenation is the best immediate protection for the fetus." }, { population: "Opioid dependence or chronic pain", note: "The lowest effective clinical reversal may reduce abrupt withdrawal, but lay rescue devices are given as labeled and adequate ventilation remains the immediate endpoint." }],
