      aliases: ["Bridion", "Bridian", "sugamadex", "suggamadex", "rocuronium reversal", "vecuronium antidote"],
      brandExamples: ["BRIDION"],
      class: "Selective relaxant-binding agent for aminosteroid neuromuscular blockade reversal",
      usedToTreat: "Reversal of rocuronium- or vecuronium-induced neuromuscular blockade during surgery. It does not reverse succinylcholine, cisatracurium, opioid sedation, or neurologic weakness.",
      description: "Sugammadex is a modified gamma-cyclodextrin that binds rocuronium and vecuronium in plasma. The dose is chosen from the measured depth of blockade and actual body weight, not from a vague statement that surgery is over. Labeled dosing is 2 mg/kg when the second train-of-four response has reappeared, 4 mg/kg at one to two post-tetanic counts with no train-of-four response, and 16 mg/kg for immediate reversal about 3 minutes after rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg. Because the product removes free aminosteroid relaxant rather than stimulating acetylcholine, it avoids the muscarinic mechanism of neostigmine but has distinct anaphylaxis, bradycardia, recurrence, renal, and contraception risks.",
      mechanism: "Sugammadex forms a tight water-soluble inclusion complex around free rocuronium or vecuronium. Lowering the free plasma concentration creates a gradient that draws relaxant away from the neuromuscular junction, so nicotinic receptors become available and muscle transmission returns. The mechanism is molecule-specific; it cannot encapsulate succinylcholine or benzylisoquinolinium agents such as cisatracurium. Underdosing at deep blockade can leave enough free drug for recurrence, which is why objective monitoring and depth-matched dosing matter even when movement appears to return.",
